# 0006. Secrets never in the repo: untracked secrets file, global gitleaks hook

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-11

## Context
The old `.zshrc` held API keys (an OpenAI key in early history had to be revoked). A public
dotfiles repo must not leak credentials, in this repo or any other.

## Decision
- Machine-only settings and credentials live in untracked `~/.config/zsh/{local,secrets}.zsh`,
  sourced last by `conf.d/90-local.zsh`; `make secrets` / `migrate-secrets.sh` move them out of an
  old `.zshrc`.
- Git uses a global `core.hooksPath`; every hook is a symlink to `git/hooks/_dispatch`, which runs
  gitleaks on staged changes at pre-commit, then the repo's own `.git/hooks/<name>`.
- `--no-verify` and `SKIP_GITLEAKS` are not used for routine work.

## Alternatives
- Per-repo pre-commit framework: easy to forget in a new repo.
- Secrets in a password manager CLI at startup: a process and possibly network per shell.

## Consequences
- Every repo on the machine is scanned without setup.
- Repos using the pre-commit framework need `git config --local core.hooksPath .git/hooks`.
- Without gitleaks installed, commits only warn.

## References
`git/hooks/_dispatch`, `conf.d/90-local.zsh`, `.gitleaksignore`, CLAUDE.md gotcha 6.
